Z||000000||Fiasco||04-12-2000||04:33 PM||||Happiness is sucking air into the master cylinder after the last pump of your 2 hour clutch bleed!!! Then you start over, swearing to never make that same idiot mistake again. Then you go ahead and make that same idiot mistake again.  -4 hours later, and she shifts like a charm.<P>Somebody please remind me how much I love my Fiero!||||reg||8
Z||000001||Phantom Rage||04-12-2000||05:15 PM||||Sorry to hear, I've done that a few times myself.  Here's a tip that I learned from this forum, it's called a gravity bleed.  Just jack up the front of the car where your master cyl is and make sure it is higher than your slave, then start to pour away keeping your master cylinder topped off, while gravity pulls the fluid into an out of your slave.  Close your bleader screw when the clutch fluid runs clear, test your system and then your done.  This should work given that you have no leaks in your system.<BR>Hope this will help the next time you work on system.<BR> <BR><p>[This message has been edited by Phantom Rage (edited 04-12-2000).]||||reg||12
Z||000002||Fiasco||04-12-2000||08:10 PM||||Thanks.<BR>I'm not sure I understand, though...<BR>If the goal is to get air out of the hydraulic system, isn't raising the front of the vehicle going to persuade some bubbles to stay in the system instead of heading toward the bleeder?<BR>I actually raised the rear to help the air in that direction, since it would rather go uphill.<p>[This message has been edited by Fiasco (edited 04-12-2000).]||||reg||5
Z||000003||Phantom Rage||04-13-2000||11:27 PM||||Doh!!! forgot to mention you should also pull back on the slave cyl rod(toward bleeder screw)as far as it will go and hold it while a friend closes the bleeder screw.  <P>Good point!  I don't know why this works all I know is it worked like a charm on my first try.  You can find a better description in Archies V8, archisms(sp?)||||reg||1
